---
// Fact-forward pass (matches the corrected helical-piers-deep-dive.astro
// treatment) -- content re-verified against MFRC's push-piers page and
// rewritten in TLS's own structure/voice rather than following its
// layout. No misconceptions/Q&A section (dropped sitewide per Tim's
// direction that the format itself talks down to a reader who's
// probably already done real research) -- the facts that section used
// to carry are already stated once, plainly, in the sections below.
// [TIM: ...] marks spots needing real NM numbers/field experience
// instead of guesses.
//
// Per Tim: added the real reason push piers are TLS's least-installed
// method -- not a lesser option, but a genuine mechanical limit. Skin
// friction increases with depth; once it approaches the dead load
// available to react against, the ram lifts the house instead of
// advancing the pile. NM's dominant monolithic-turndown-slab foundation
// often doesn't carry enough dead load per linear foot to avoid that.
// TLS's working threshold is roughly 3,000-4,000 lbs/linear ft before
// selecting push piers, with an exception when loose soil sits over a
// shallow, very hard layer at a known depth -- then wall load matters
// less.
//
// Installation-section placeholder now filled too: pier-and-beam fails
// the same dead-load test above; older adobe fails for a different
// reason entirely -- the structure flexes slightly during installation
// since the ram reacts off the building, and adobe is brittle but
// strong (carries load fine, doesn't tolerate movement) -- so a method
// that installs independent of the structure (helical pier, micropile)
// is the better fit there.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Push Piers in New Mexico | TLS Foundations';
const summary =
  'Push piers -- also called resistance piers -- are steel pipe sections hydraulically pressed from a bracket at an existing footing, using the structure\'s own weight as the reaction force. Capacity comes from end bearing and shaft friction in compression; installation pressure is useful field data but is not, by itself, proof of long-term capacity the way a soil investigation is. The reaction requirement is what actually decides whether a given structure can take them -- light wood-frame construction often cannot supply it.';
const description =
  'What push piers are, how they carry load using the structure\'s own weight, and when New Mexico construction does -- and does not -- call for them.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Push Piers in New Mexico',
    description,
    about: description,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/push-piers-deep-dive',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Foundation Repair Methods', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foundation-repair-methods' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Push Piers', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/push-piers-deep-dive' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="minimal"
  schema={schema}
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Push Piers"
  nextPage={{ path: '/foundation-repair-methods', name: 'Foundation Repair Methods' }}
>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Push Piers</span>
        <h1>Push Piers —<br /><em>What the Reaction Force Means</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Also called resistance piers. The pier doesn't push itself into the ground — the structure does. That one mechanical fact decides which buildings can actually take them.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">A push pier is a steel shaft pressed into the ground using the structure's own weight as reaction. That single mechanism — reaction supplied by the building, not by torque or drilling — is what a push pier is and what it requires to work at all.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>The Bracket Does the Pushing, <em>Not the Pier</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A push pier — also called a resistance pier — is a steel pipe section advanced into the ground from a bracket mounted at an existing footing.</p>
          <p>The name describes the installation itself: the existing structure supplies the reaction that presses the pier downward, section by section, as they're joined together. That's a fundamentally different mechanism from a helical pier, which is rotated in under torque, or a micropile, which is drilled and grouted. A push pier system is three parts — steel pipe sections, a foundation bracket at the footing, and a hydraulic ram using the building's own weight as reaction.</p>
          <p>Of everything in TLS's toolkit, push piers are typically the least-installed method in New Mexico — not because they're a lesser option, but because the mechanism itself has a real limit, and the region's dominant foundation type runs into it more often than the others do.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>End Bearing, Shaft Friction, and <em>What Pressure Readings Don't Prove</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Once installed, a push pier is meant to carry part of the foundation load through the bracket and steel section down to a deeper bearing layer, or to a depth where enough resistance has developed.</p>
          <p>Installation pressure is useful field information while it's happening, but it isn't by itself proof of long-term capacity — the footing, the bracket, pier alignment, the soil profile, groundwater, and the structural load path all factor in too. It's also worth being precise about the vocabulary: pressure readings on a push pier are not the same thing as torque on a helical pier, and shouldn't be treated as interchangeable data.</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Compression vs. tension</span>
            <p>Push piers develop most of their capacity in compression, through end bearing and shaft friction. Resisting tension or uplift isn't typical of standard hardware — it requires a specifically engineered bracket or coupler detail that most installations don't include. If a job needs uplift resistance (wind, expansive-soil heave), that's a different conversation before piers are even proposed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">The reaction limit</span>
            <p>Because a push pier uses the structure's own dead load as reaction, there's a real ceiling on how deep it can go before the mechanism works against itself. As the pile advances, skin friction along its length increases; once that resistance approaches the dead load available to react against it, the hydraulic ram starts lifting the house rather than driving the pile further — before it's reached full target depth. New Mexico's dominant foundation type, the monolithic turndown slab, often doesn't carry enough dead load per linear foot of wall to avoid that limit, which is the real reason push piers see less use here than the other piering options — not because they don't work, but because the structure itself often can't supply what the mechanism needs.</p>
          </div>
          <details class="deeper">
            <summary class="deeper-toggle">
              <span>Going Deeper — For the Technically Curious</span>
              <span class="deeper-toggle-icon">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="deeper-body">
              <p>Push pier systems are typically evaluated under ICC-ES AC517 (Acceptance Criteria for Push Pier Foundation Systems) — that's the mechanism that pulls a hydraulically-jacked pile into the same code category as a driven pile, since the IBC itself doesn't use the term "push pier." Once that classification applies, <strong>IBC Section 1705.7</strong>, Driven Deep Foundations, governs installation and requires continuous special inspection: verifying material, size, and length; monitoring installation pressure and depth of penetration; recording tip elevation; and confirming the element reaches its design capacity.</p>
              <p>As with helical piers, that inspection is a real cost the homeowner bears, and it isn't always requested at the full code standard on residential retrofit work — code text doesn't carve out an exception for repair versus new construction, but enforcement in the field is less consistent than the code language alone would suggest.</p>
              <p class="deeper-cite">Source: IBC Section 1705.7, Driven Deep Foundations; ICC-ES AC517.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="scope">
          <h2>What Rules a Push Pier <em>Out</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">An active plumbing leak, poor drainage, irrigation, or expansive-soil moisture change that's still unaddressed.</p>
          <p>A lightly loaded interior slab rather than an actual foundation load path. A project that needs uplift or tension resistance without an engineered bracket or coupler built for it. A wall load lighter than roughly 3,000 to 4,000 pounds per linear foot — TLS's general threshold before push piers are the correct method, since anything lighter risks the reaction limit above.</p>
          <p>The one real exception is loose soil sitting over a relatively shallow but very hard layer. When the target depth is known and consistent — piers reaching a hard stopping layer at a predictable depth, for example — wall load matters less, since the piers aren't fighting increasing skin friction the whole way down. Knowing the depth and having enough wall load to reach it is what matters at that point, not the wall load figure on its own.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>Where the Work <em>Happens</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Installation happens at the footing — excavation to expose it, the bracket mounted, then pipe sections hydraulically pressed and joined one at a time using the building's weight as reaction.</p>
          <p>Two New Mexico construction types are usually the wrong fit for push piers, for two different reasons. Pier-and-beam foundations don't carry enough wall load to supply reliable reaction — the same dead-load ceiling covered above, just a construction type where it's almost always a problem rather than an occasional one. Older adobe construction is a different issue entirely: installing a push pier means the structure flexes slightly as the ram reacts against it, and adobe walls are brittle but strong — able to carry real load, but not built to tolerate movement without cracking. If the wall above a footing can't absorb any installation-induced movement without risking a crack, that's a sign to use a method that installs independent of the structure instead — a helical pier or a micropile, neither of which reacts off the building the way a push pier does.</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#push-pier" class="body-link">Glossary: Push Pier →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

